{"id":"u-6916d122-b4e6-475e-85d5-7eb12cae8df6","src":"community","title":"The Weimer","author":"Kyler Weimer","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/QoLQss-the-weimer","desc":"The Weimer Method\n\n“Hammer the Iron Until the Iron Breaks First.”\n\nThe Weimer Method is a 6-week high-volume strength cycle built for lifters who want brutal strength, dense muscle, and unstoppable work capacity without grinding themselves into dust. Inspired by the bones of 5/3/1 but rebuilt into something heavier, meaner, and more relentless, the Weimer doubles the exposure to every phase — double the 5x5 punishment, double the 3x3 pressure, and double the war against 90%. More time under heavy weight. More chances to adapt. More chances to prove you belong under the bar.\n\nEvery major lift ends with an all-out top set marked with a target rep goal. Those sets are the soul of the program — controlled aggression, calculated violence, and absolute commitment to moving weight no matter what the bar says. The split runs 2 upper days and 2 lower days, with one day dedicated to crushing squats and the other built entirely around raw deadlift strength. Volume is balanced just enough to keep running and conditioning alive while the main goal stays the same: becoming brutally strong.\n\nThe final two weeks pull back accessory fatigue and sharpen recovery so when the heavy weights come, you attack them fresh, explosive, and dangerous. \n\nThe name comes from the German phrase “Wiederstand durch Wiederholung” — strength through repeated resistance — because strength is not built in comfort. It’s forged by surviving the weight long enough to command it.\n\nYEEEEEAAAAAAGHHHHHHBHH\n\n\nYour 90% sets are the proving ground of the Weimer Method. The final top set of each main lift is pushed for as many reps as possible with a clear target number — not to fail recklessly, but to force adaptation under real weight. Use this quick estimate to calculate your new max after your AMRAP set:\n\nIf your estimated max climbs, increase your training max for the next cycle and run it again. That’s the core of the Weimer Method — endless progression through repeated assault on the fundamentals. Because the volume waves intelligently, the accessory fatigue drops in the final weeks, and the split stays recoverable, the program is designed to be run indefinitely. Cycle after cycle, the weight gets heavier, the reps get cleaner, and the lifter gets harder to kill.","level":["Intermediate"],"goals":["Muscle","Strength"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":70,"joins":1,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-05-27T13:00:31.827Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Upper bench focus","Squat focus leg day","Bench 2","Deadlifts"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":5,"pct":70,"x":4},{"r":10,"pct":70}]},{"n":"Bench Press (Wide Grip)","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"pct":65,"x":3}]},{"n":"Chest Fly (Machine)","sets":[{"r":20,"rpe":8,"x":5}]},{"n":"Pullover (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"lo":15,"hi":20,"rpe":8,"x":3},{"lo":20,"hi":25,"rpe":10}]},{"n":"Chest Supported Row (Machine)","sets":[{"lo":15,"hi":20,"pct":80,"x":5}]},{"n":"Bicep Curl (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":100}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":5,"pct":75,"x":5}]},{"n":"Squat (Paused)","sets":[{"r":10,"pct":50,"x":3}]},{"n":"Hack Squat","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":8,"x":4}]},{"n":"Single Leg Press","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":8,"x":3},{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":10}]},{"n":"Lateral Raise (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":20,"rpe":8,"x":5}]},{"n":"Rear Delt Fly (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":20,"rpe":8,"x":5}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Bench Press (Close Grip)","sets":[{"r":5,"pct":75,"x":4},{"r":10,"pct":75}]},{"n":"Bench Press (Paused)","sets":[{"r":5,"pct":75,"x":3}]},{"n":"JM Press","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":20,"rpe":7,"x":3}]},{"n":"T-Bar Row","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":20,"rpe":8,"x":5}]},{"n":"Chin-Up (Bodyweight)","sets":[{"r":50}]},{"n":"Dip (Bodyweight)","sets":[{"r":50}]}]},{"ex":[{"n":"Deadlift (Barbell)","sets":[